Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) reveals that LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with instance degeneracy across datasets.
Under standard early stopping, Few-NERD degeneracy is 13.8\% (vs.\ SciERC's 17.8\%, CoNLL's 47.4\%; training convergence modulates this rate, Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}), and LP achieves highly significant selection gains ($+1.36$~pp, 95\% CI $[+1.18, +1.53]$, $p{<}0.001$), while all other signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$, FK $-2.26$, EM $-3.85$~pp).

\paragraph{Two necessary conditions for LP selection.}
LP selection requires both low degeneracy and adequate LP-quality alignment.
The cross-dataset gradient is monotonic (47.4\% $\to$ fail, 17.8\% $\to$ $+0.42$~pp, 13.8\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), and degeneracy below ${\sim}$15\% consistently enables positive LP gains.
However, low degeneracy alone is not sufficient: the LoRA-rank ablation (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) reduces SciERC degeneracy to 7.2\% with wider LP spread (median range 0.057 vs.\ 0.041 nats), yet LP selection still fails because within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$ remains weak.
Few-NERD, where LP succeeds, satisfies both conditions: low degeneracy (13.8\%) and strong alignment ($\rho{\approx}0.5$).
Per-type analysis within Few-NERD confirms the pattern (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\paragraph{The correlation-selection gap persists for structural signals.}
All five signals achieve positive quality estimation on Few-NERD (LP $\rho{=}0.42$, VC $0.39$, FK $0.31$, SJ $0.30$, EM $0.29$), yet only LP translates QE into selection.
The gap between VC's quality estimation ($\rho{=}0.39$) and its selection outcome ($-1.79$~pp) confirms that the gap is not an artifact of weak QE: even strong instance-level QE signals cannot rank candidates within an instance.
Within Few-NERD, 7 of 8 entity types show positive LP selection gains ($+0.23$ to $+4.73$~pp), the sole exception being the highest-degeneracy type (Art, 19.8\%; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain at two granularities. Blue circles: cross-dataset aggregates ($n{=}3$, exploratory). Orange triangles: per-entity-type within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, controlled). The dashed line at ${\sim}$15\%\protect\footnotemark{} separates a regime where LP selection improves over greedy (left) from one where it fails (right). The cross-dataset monotonic trend is suggestive ($n{=}3$) but not statistically testable; the within-dataset entity-type evidence provides rigorous confirmation under controlled conditions.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

\footnotetext{Empirically, the boundary falls between Few-NERD Person (13.4\%, LP $+1.25$~pp) and Art (19.8\%, LP $-0.17$~pp); we use ${\sim}$15\% as an approximate characterization.}

Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter} visualizes the relationship at two granularities.
The cross-dataset monotonic trend is suggestive ($n{=}3$) but not statistically testable; the within-dataset entity-type evidence provides rigorous confirmation under controlled conditions ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, $n{=}8$).

\paragraph{LP selection scales with sample budget in the low-degeneracy regime.}
On the same 4{,}351 Few-NERD instances, doubling $N$ from 8 to 16 yields a significant additional $+0.52$~pp (paired bootstrap $p{=}0.003$, 95\% CI $[+0.17, +0.87]$): LP selection improves from $+1.37$~pp to $+1.88$~pp (both $p{<}0.001$).
The gain is driven by reduced degeneracy (14.6\% $\to$ 7.0\%) expanding oracle headroom (12.1 $\to$ 15.4~pp), while LP ranking quality remains stable ($\rho{\approx}0.62$). Signal quality is thus model-intrinsic rather than $N$-dependent.
At $N{=}16$, all 8 entity types show positive LP gains, including types that were negative at $N{=}8$.
Structural signals remain uniformly negative at $N{=}16$ (SJ $-1.36$, FK $-1.47$~pp); the correlation-selection gap for consensus-based methods persists regardless of sample budget.

